--TEST--
A capture whose enum admits two cases carrying one written value is refused, because that segment names both — `rule:routing/an-enum-capture-is-spelled-by-its-backing-value-or-its-case-name`, `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The alias itself is legal and stays legal: equality over an enum is value
// equality, so there is no identity for two names to collide on
// (`rule:enums/declaration`), and under the case-name spelling the two names
// are still distinct. It is the value spelling that cannot carry it -- every
// case here wrote its value, so a segment is that integer, and `/p/7` would
// name both cases at once. Refused rather than first-wins: a match that picked
// either would be choosing for the program.
enum Lang: uint { De = 7, Deutsch = 7 }

class Docs {
    #[Core\Route(path: "/p/{lang}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Docs::page")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function page(Lang $lang): string { return "page"; }
}
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0819]: `Lang::De` and `Lang::Deutsch` both carry `7`, so that segment names two cases
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
